wasm3 for Onyx
==============

The WebAssembly interpreter of NetSurf's JavaScript (content/handlers/javascript/quickjs/
qjs_wasm.c: the WebAssembly namespace; docs/06-JET-BROWSER.md section 21).

Upstream: https://github.com/wasm3/wasm3 (MIT, LICENSE here), the `main` branch at commit
28ecb9a (2026-09-29, "Implement Atomics"), which says it is version 0.9.2 (after the
0.9.1-beta.1 tag): per-module memories and the linking of a module's memory / table /
global / function imports to another module's exports in the same runtime, resource caps
(a runtime's memory), the exception handling API. Its spec suite (wg-3.0: 27878 tests) passes
with the settings below (run on the PC with upstream's test/run-spec-test.py).

Kept: the engine only -- source/ as src/ (without the WASI, libc and tracer host modules,
m3_api_*, nor m3_wasi.h), LICENSE. Not kept: the CLI, the platforms, the tests, the docs.

Why wasm3 (and not WAMR's fast interpreter): portable C99 with no platform layer to write
(WAMR needs its OS adaptation -- mmap, threads, a native stack guard -- and is several times
larger), fast for an interpreter (its operations are threaded by tail calls: -O3), small
(~200 KB of AArch64 code), and its API gives what the JavaScript API needs (raw host
functions, several modules in one runtime linked to one another, memories one can grow).
WAMR's AOT / JIT would be the next step for speed.

Onyx changes (marked "Onyx:"):

- src/m3_onyx_config.h (new), included first by src/m3_config.h: the build's settings --
  no stack switching, snapshots, typed function references, gas metering (the page's
  scripts do not use them; they are the largest parts), no guarded memories (the Pi has no
  mmap; the bench the same), 1.5 MB of native stack for a module's recursion, linear
  memories of at most 1 GiB (a store's are capped at 512 MB by qjs_wasm.c).
- src/m3_validate.c: an opcode the compiler has no operation for (the 0xFD SIMD prefix)
  is refused by the validator instead of skipped: WebAssembly.validate() answers for the
  whole module before anything is compiled (pages detect SIMD that way).
- m3_Yield (weak in src/m3_core.c) is defined by qjs_wasm.c: a module that runs past the
  page's script time traps.

Build: user/netsurf/Makefile makes libm3.a (committed) with the Pi's toolchain, -O3;
tools/tests/netsurf/host.mk compiles the same sources for the PC bench.
